./results/telemetry/owner-audits/audit-full.json:513:   "argument": "I attacked the \"deleting the count loses nothing\" claim eight ways; every attack failed against file:line evidence, and one attack failed so badly it inverts into positive support for deletion.\n\nA. \"A [codex-*-fix] commit can land after the review without invalidating it.\" FAILS. Gate 4 requires a review whose `commit_id == head_sha` AND whose body carries `<!-- mipstarre-review pr=N head=SHA -->` (pr_merge.py:171-176); `gh_common.post_review` only ever creates a review with `commit_id` set to the reviewed SHA and never PATCHes a body across SHAs (gh_common.py:242-260). Gate 2 requires the local branch tip to equal the head SHA (pr_merge.py:139-143), gate 2b re-fetches and requires the head to contain the live base tip (pr_merge.py:~300-312), and `merge_pr` merges through the REST `sha` guard with a merged read-back (gh_common.py:277-290). review.sh additionally re-checks `head_moved` and publishes NOTHING if the head moved while the reviewer was thinking (review.sh:~1010-1016).\n\nB. \"CI statuses on the head can be stale or forged.\" FAILS. ci.sh refuses to publish unless the worktree HEAD is exactly the GitHub head (ci.sh:763), publishes nothing at all for partial `--only/--skip-build/--base` runs (ci.sh:527-535, 761), dies on a dirty worktree before publishing (ci.sh:764-767) and withholds `local-ci/summary` if the tree became dirty mid-run (ci.sh:1229-1233). `skipped -> success` (ci.sh:1178) is derived from a real `merge-base..head` diff (ci.sh:586, 601, 1028), and statuses are per-SHA, with autofix stamping `pending` on every new SHA (autofix.sh:781-786).\n\nC. \"The bot-commit skip lets an unreviewed fix commit merge.\" This is the one real hole in the ladder (review.sh:428-435 exits 0 without publishing when the head subject is bot-prefixed) \u2014 but it fails CLOSED at gate 4, not at gate 6: no review on the head means refusal (pr_merge.py:171-176). The count is not what protects it.\n\nD. \"Hand-ticked or carried-over findings fake a clean ledger.\" FAILS. Findings never cross SHAs (review.md:270-283), `preserve_prior` deliberately does not merge ledgers (review.sh:633-637), and gate 4 demands BOTH a clean ledger and `local-review/summary == success`, which review.sh computes from the same run (review.sh:1030-1043, pr_merge.py:180-186).\n\nE. \"Autofix races pr_merge.\" FAILS \u2014 gate 6(a) is kept (pr_merge.py:212-222) and the REST sha guard is atomic.\n\nF. \"The cap is an independent second opinion on the loop.\" FAILS, decisively and in reverse: autofix.sh:60 and pr_merge.py:236 read the SAME `MIPSTARRE_FIX_CAP`. Raising it raises both at once, so gate 6(b) never was an independent bound on anything.\n\nG. Coverage: the review that greenlights the head diffs the FULL `MERGE_BASE..HEAD_SHA` range (review.sh:489-494), so every fix commit's content is inside the approving review no matter how many there were. The count adds no content-coverage property.\n\nH. Reachability. With a constant cap, autofix cannot produce more than FIX_CAP fix commits: the count is re-derived from branch history at startup (autofix.sh:401-408) and re-checked before EVERY phase (autofix.sh:695-696). So gate 6(b) can only ever fire on commits the fix loop did not make \u2014 and PR #5 proves exactly that. All six `[codex-review-fix]` commits on `issue-0006-qpbt-minimal-skeleton` (b5da371, 99ccb0c, f36df43, 0aacbca, 3b394f2, 356ec27) are authored AND committed by `Dengnifer <ruixuan.deng@icloud.com>` with no `Machine-generated by local/bin/autofix.sh` trailer; autofix's own commits are made as `codex[bot]` (autofix.sh:73-74, 770-775). autofix.sh never ran on this branch; its cap never fired. Gate 6(b) is measuring an operator-driven repair that borrowed the bot subject prefix, not a runaway fix loop.\n\nLive evidence on head b5da371de32d: all eight `local-ci/<step>` plus `local-ci/summary` are `success`, `local-review/summary` is `success`, and the marker-bound COMMENT review on that exact commit id reads `VERDICT: APPROVED (code=APPROVED, prose=n/a)` with 0 unchecked findings. Gate 6(b) is the only refusal, and its message (\"needs human attention, not another merge attempt\") is a pure owner-stall.\n\nWhat IS genuinely lost is not safety but observability: autofix.md:181-184 calls a cap hit \"protocol evidence\" for telemetry, and after deletion nothing at merge time flags an operator-driven review->fix->review cascade (each round of which, in PR #5, needed `review.sh --force-review` to get past review.sh:428-435 \u2014 four such forced reviews exist on this PR, against review.md:145-149 which reserves `--force-review` for the single terminal review at the cap). That is a cost/termination and record-keeping concern, not a soundness one: an unbounded cascade never merges anything unsound, it only burns time, and review.md \u00a712's round cap plus `--adjudicated` (pr_merge.py:188-203) already handles non-convergence without the owner.",
./results/telemetry/owner-audits/audit-full.json:520:   "argument": "NOT REFUTED from the process side. Every protocol attack I ran died on the same fact: the merge-time cap is nowhere named as a guard, and the guards that ARE named live elsewhere.\n\nWHAT DESIGN.md:70-72 ACTUALLY SAYS (the gate's own citation, and it is a miscitation). /home/drx/MIPStarRE-qpbt/local/DESIGN.md:70-72 is the tail of invariant 2: \"review, which then publishes nothing at all \u2014 the *absence* of a green `local-review/summary` is the block, never a silent skip. Bot commits with prefix `[codex-auto-fix]`/`[codex-review-fix]` are not re-reviewed except the final fix at the iteration cap, which gets one forced review.\" That is about the review SKIP, not about merging. The \"combined iteration cap (default 5) across all fix kinds\" text the gate message paraphrases is invariant 3, at DESIGN.md:74-76 \u2014 which says \"Serialized fixes... combined iteration cap... sync/audit CI failures are never auto-fixed\", i.e. a property of the FIX LOOP. So gate 6's refusal cites lines that do not support it.\n\nATTACK 1 \u2014 \"the cap is the mechanism forcing human review of a churning PR.\" Fails, and inverts. review.md:124-136 enumerates the ping-pong guard as exactly three interlocking mechanisms: (1) the bot-commit skip in review.sh, (2) \"The combined iteration cap (`autofix.md` \u00a75)\", (3) exclusion of sync/audit failures from auto-fix. All three are loop-side; pr_merge.py is not one of them, and review.md \u00a75 cites autofix.md for the cap, not the merge gate. autofix.md:160-170 defines the cap ritual (human-attention note, forced review, exit 0) as firing AT count == cap, i.e. at 5 \u2014 which pr_merge.py:237 permits (`iterations > cap`). So the protocol's own \"human attention required\" moment already happens one commit BEFORE the merge refusal, and merging at 5 is allowed. The refusal at 6 is not the enforcement of \"a human takes over\"; it fires after that note already exists and after CI+review went green. Worse, as a churn guard it has inverted incentives: refusing the merge is the one action that prolongs the churn.\n\nATTACK 2 \u2014 \"deletion lets a merge skip a required adjudication.\" Fails. review.md:379-393 (\u00a712, dated 2026-08-30) makes the round cap permissive \u2014 \"the operator MAY close the loop by adjudication instead of iteration\" \u2014 and adjudication is only ever needed when the verdict is adverse; gate 4 (pr_merge.py:184-203) still demands either a clean exact-head verdict or an ADJUDICATION comment naming that head. Nothing conditions adjudication on fix-commit count. And the adjudicator is the operator by design: personas/main.md:16 (\"you... merge through the gate... and adjudicate\") and main.md step 5 (\"at most FOUR full rounds, then \u00a712 operator adjudication\"). EVOLUTION.md:150-153 says \u00a712 is the ANALOGUE of the bot-fix cap, not a consumer of it.\n\nATTACK 3 \u2014 \"unreviewed code lands.\" Fails on code. review.sh:489-493 builds the review diff as merge_base..HEAD_SHA, so every published review covers the whole PR, not the last commit; review.md:271-275 confirms findings are re-derived per head. Gate 4 requires a marker review bound to the exact head. So the merged bytes are always reviewed whatever the fix count.\n\nATTACK 4 \u2014 the one that has real teeth, and it is about implementation, not the thesis. pr_merge.py:316-317: `merge_base = check_fix_gates(...)` then `check_dependencies(..., merge_base, head_sha)`, and gate 7 scans commit messages with `git log --format=%B merge_base..head` under `check=False` (pr_merge.py:266-267) \u2014 it swallows its own error. The fail-closed base resolution (\"no local ref resolves for base\", pr_merge.py:225-230) lives inside the block being deleted, and EVOLUTION.md:316-317 records it as a bypass closed by PR 7's review round 3 (\"The fix-iteration cap fails closed on an unresolvable merge base rather than counting zero fixes\"). Deleting (b) \"entirely\" would delete it and let gate 7 silently scan an empty range, merging a PR that auto-closes an issue with open sub-issues. Fixable by amendment, not a defect of the idea.\n\nWHAT IS GENUINELY LOST, and why it is a reporting function rather than a gate. The rationale's premise \u2014 MIPSTARRE_FIX_CAP survives \"as the loop-termination backstop\" \u2014 does not hold for the case that triggered this. `git log` on branch issue-0006-qpbt-minimal-skeleton shows the six commits (b5da371, 99ccb0c, f36df43, 0aacbca, 3b394f2, 356ec27) are all authored AND committed by `Dengnifer <ruixuan.deng@icloud.com>` with bespoke subjects; autofix.sh commits as `codex[bot]` (autofix.sh:70) with exactly three fixed subjects (autofix.sh:805, :831, :859) and refuses at `FIX_ITERATIONS >= FIX_CAP` before every phase (autofix.sh:695). So autofix.sh never made these and its cap can never bound them \u2014 for this class of commit the \"backstop\" bounds nothing, and gate 6(b) is today the sole mechanical detector that fix-prefixed (hence rung-7 review-suppressing, review.md:53) commits are being minted by hand and cleared with repeated `--force-review`, which review.md:146-147 explicitly forbids (\"Do not use it to 'just get a review' of a bot commit; that reopens the cascade one commit at a time\"). Combined with review.md \u00a712's four-round cap having zero code enforcement, deleting (b) leaves round/churn bounding entirely to operator self-discipline. That is a real loss \u2014 but of a detector aimed at the wrong actor at the wrong time, not of a safety property: the content that merges is still proven green and reviewed on the exact SHA.\n\nFinally, the standing texts point the same way as the proposal on the interruption itself: ~/.codex/prompts/goal.md limits owner escalation to \"credentials, a scope-control budget ceiling, or a mathematical design decision you cannot resolve\" \u2014 a green PR at six fix commits is none of these; and local/README.md:31-32 describes the gate to operators as refusing \"on red CI, missing review, or unresolved findings\", never on a fix count. But goal.md also says, in the same list, \"Merge only through `local/bin/pr_merge.py`; never push to main directly; never weaken a gate to pass it\", and notes the predecessor session was terminated for self-granting an override. So the operator must not delete this itself while blocked by it: it needs an owner-approved amendment, which is exactly what this review is for.",
./results/telemetry/owner-audits/audit-full.json:527:   "argument": "ATTACK FAILED \u2014 no research-data consumer reads the merge-time count, and the statistic is fully reconstructible without it.\n\n1) pr_merge.py emits NO telemetry at all. It writes no jsonl, calls no telemetry.py, appends nothing. Grepping the file for telemetry/record/jsonl returns only docstring text and the `merge_base` variable. The count exists only as a printed line, `pr_merge.py:242` `passed(f\"gate 6 fix loop idle, {iterations}/{cap} fix commits\")`, and it is discarded \u2014 nothing parses `--check-only` stdout (grep for \"check-only\"/\"gate 6\" across all of local/ hits only `pr_merge.py:36,108,220,229,238,242,459`; no shell or python caller).\n\n2) No export/summary path touches it. `local/bin/telemetry.py` writes only sessions.jsonl / stages.jsonl / builds.jsonl / events.md (`telemetry.py:632 cmd_stage`, `:653 cmd_build`, `:685 cmd_event`, `:508 cmd_session_summarize`); none of those schemas carries a fix-count field (verified against the live records in `results/telemetry/*.jsonl`). Its only script caller is `dispatch.sh:243`. The github-sync snapshot is `gh_common.py:399-407` \u2014 open issues + open pulls + three counters, no closed PRs, no fix counts, and it never invokes pr_merge.py. `local/bin/export_issues.py` does not exist (the hypothesized consumer isn't there); `results/telemetry/reviews/` holds only `pr7-<sha>.md` verdict files with no writer referencing it.\n\n3) The \"fix iterations per PR\" statistic is preserved by git history alone, permanently. The count is defined purely by commit subject prefix in all three places (`pr_merge.py:67-69`, `autofix.sh:404-409`, and `autofix.md:146-149` states the design explicitly: \"The count is derived from the branch's own history, not from a stored field... the history is the counter\"). Merges are true two-parent merges \u2014 `gh_common.py:280` sends `\"merge_method\": \"merge\"` and `:293-297` hard-verifies \"two parents with the frozen head second\" \u2014 so every `[codex-*-fix]` commit stays reachable from main forever, regardless of the tail's branch deletion. I reconstructed the statistic live on ghz from main alone: `for m in $(git log main --merges --format=%h); do git log --format=%s $m^1..$m^2 | grep -c 'codex-auto-fix\\|codex-review-fix'; done` \u2014 and independently recovered PR #5's count as exactly 6 from `git merge-base main b5da371..b5da371`. Nothing about that depends on pr_merge.py running.\n\n4) autofix.sh independently preserves the loop record: it recomputes the identical count at `autofix.sh:404-409`, fails closed on an unresolvable merge base (`:410-412`, the round-3-F3 hardening EVOLUTION.md:316 documents), stamps `Iteration: N of M (combined cap)` into each commit body it authors (`autofix.sh:762-765`), and runs the cap ritual with the durable `<!-- autofix:cap-reached -->` PR comment (`autofix.sh:561-580`). The research duty itself lives there, not in the merge gate: `autofix.md:181-183` \u2014 \"A PR that reaches the cap is evidence... Record it in results/telemetry/events.md; repeated cap hits... are the raw material for an amendment in EVOLUTION.md.\" Keeping MIPSTARRE_FIX_CAP in autofix.sh keeps that duty and DESIGN.md invariant 3 (\"Serialized fixes... combined iteration cap (default 5)\") intact, since invariant 3 is about the fix loop, not the merge gate.\n\nRESIDUAL (an annotation gap, not a data loss). PR #5's six fix commits were authored by `Dengnifer <ruixuan.deng@icloud.com>`, not `codex[bot]` (`autofix.sh:70-71`), and carry no `Iteration: N of M` trailer \u2014 i.e. they were operator hand-fixes that never ran through autofix.sh, so autofix.sh's cap ritual never fired. In that hand-fix regime, `pr_merge.py:237-241` was the only tripwire that noticed, and it is what prompted the events.md bullet at `results/telemetry/events.md:177-186`. That is a detection prompt, not a data source: the number was recomputed from the branch, and can be recomputed at any time after merge. It argues for turning the refusal into a recorded observation, not for keeping the stall.",
