./results/telemetry/owner-audits/reviewer-assessment.json:1016:     "argument": "Every attack landed; three are fatal as specified.\n\n**1. The motivating premise is factually false, and the outcome evidence now contradicts it.** The proposal says \"across five published reviews not one carries severity advisory\" and calls PR 21 \"a 13-finding advisory-grade review.\" PR 21's review has since completed and published. `~/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews/pr21/7f5c58ba...-combined.md` is **2 blocker + 10 changes + 1 advisory** \u2014 F1 `pr_merge.py:27` (retiring gate 6 removes the autofix-cap backstop) and F2 `pr_merge.py:194` (`--adjudicated` accepts a prefix/substring without enforcing the \u00a712 disposition contract) are blockers against the merge gate itself. The reviewer *did* reach for `advisory` (F13, a test-isolation nit) the moment it had one to make. Apply the proposal's own rule to the PR that motivated it and the unchecked count goes 13 \u2192 12. The wall is still a wall; the claimed effect is a 7.7% reduction.\n\n**2. The `- [x]` half is a no-op at the budgeted cost, and a semantic collision at the real cost.** `review.sh:728` rebuilds every ledger entry unconditionally:\n```\nledger = [\"- [ ] F%d (%s) `%s` \u2014 %s\" % (n, sev, loc, summary) ...]\n```\n`LINE_BT`/`LINE_PL` capture the reviewer's checkbox as group(1) and never read it. The proposal's ~10 lines are all prompt text and do not touch 728, so a reviewer emitting `- [x] F13 (advisory)` still yields `- [ ] F13 (advisory)`, combine_review copies it verbatim, the `UNCHECKED` regex counts it, and `pr_merge.py:63/174` blocks. Net behavioural change: zero, plus a contract that now lies to the model about how its output is consumed. And if 728 *is* changed (unbudgeted), `[x]` collides with three live meanings: write_review's own published legend (`[x]` resolved / `[-]` outdated, does not block \u2014 a non-blocking state **already exists** and the proposal ignores it); `preserve_prior` (review.sh:633-642), which warns on `^- \\[(x|-)\\] F` to mean \"human judgements are being overwritten,\" a warning that would then fire on every same-SHA re-review; and review.md \u00a712, where \"every box ticked\" means *disposed and converted to a tracked issue*. Reviewer-ticked advisories are ticked and untracked, so nothing can tell disposition from advisory.\n\n**3. The round-3 suppression rule lets real defects merge \u2014 counterexample from the record.** PR 5 round 3 (`99ccb0c1`, 08:21) is **4 findings, all severity `changes`, zero blockers**: redundant transitive imports, an empty second public name `binaryEquiv`, a `polyFunc` abbreviation duplicating the LDT declaration *contrary to the binding brief*, and duplicated question-supports. Under \"from round 3 only blocker-severity net-new findings may be added; anything lesser goes in the ## Review prose,\" all four move to prose and the ledger becomes `<!-- no findings -->`, unresolved=0. Two branches, both worse:\n- reviewer keeps CHANGES_REQUESTED \u2192 gate 4 blocks with an **empty checklist**; the operator has nothing to tick, \u00a712 adjudication has no \"last round's findings\" to list, and convergence is impossible \u2014 churn goes up, not down;\n- reviewer downgrades to COMMENTED (which the trusted task line 101, \"advisory items alone do not block approval,\" positively invites once the ledger is empty) \u2192 `unresolved=0` + COMMENTED \u2192 review.sh posts `local-review/summary=success` and gate 4 passes. **PR 5 merges with four unfixed defects and zero tracked issues.** Round 3 is the single round in the entire corpus with no blockers, and it is the round immediately before PR 5's only-ever APPROVED (`b5da371d`, 0 findings, 08:57) \u2014 the proposal fires exactly where the loop was about to work. Same for PR 7 round 4 (`16116ab4`, 7 blocker + 10 changes): ten changes-level findings demoted to prose on the last round before adjudication, breaking \u00a712's closing invariant, \"Nothing is dropped silently: an adjudicated finding lives on as an issue\" \u2014 only ledger lines become issues.\n\n**4. The round counter is unsound as derived.** Counting marker-bound reviews counts *distinct reviewed head SHAs*, not review rounds. `post_review` (gh_common.py:242-260) is idempotent per commit_id, so same-SHA re-reviews are fine \u2014 but `autofix.sh` pushes to the PR branch (lines 590 and 875), and each push creates a new head \u2192 ci.sh \u2192 a fresh marker-bound review. A PR with two operator rounds and three autofix pushes reads as round 5: it gets the adjudication banner and the blocker-only suppression before a human has responded even twice.\n\n**5. Wrong lens for the cost claim, and it pushes the dominant cost driver the wrong way.** It touches none of the three measured drivers (20 KB `MAX_CONTEXT_BYTES` diff truncation, no wall-clock/turn budget, 83.8% of tool-output bytes outside the diff). Worse: \"this is round N of at most 4\" and \"only *net-new* findings\" both require knowing what prior rounds said, and review.sh supplies no previous ledger while the trusted task's own channels for it (`gh pr diff`, `get_review_comments`, GraphQL `reviewThreads`) are forbidden by the local contract at review.sh:634. The measured consequence of that exact gap is PR 21's 50 commands / 5.0 MB / ~19 min mining `~/.cache/mipstarre-dev/reviews` for old ledgers. This proposal strengthens the incentive on that pathology without opening the channel.\n\n**6. Latent gate break in the banner.** review.sh:869-871 documents that the gate regex is applied to \"the whole combined body, not just the ledger \u2014 so a stray `- [ ]` bullet in a reviewer's prose counts for both or neither.\" \u00a712 verbatim is numbered (1.-4.) and safe, but any bulleted paraphrase of it in a round-5 banner (\"- [ ] post an ADJUDICATION comment\") silently increments `unresolved` on a line no one can ever tick, making the PR permanently un-greenable.",
