./results/telemetry/registry-archive/prs/0001-docs-blueprint-qpbt-chapters-and-dependency-graph/reviews/4277943db23d2dde26e90908ede8041fb7902e36-code.md:39:**F1.** The source proof of `lem:ld-soundness` calls the games “identical” and invokes Theorem 4.7 immediately at [08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex](/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/.worktrees/issue-0002-qpbt-blueprint/references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:447), lines 447–454. But [Ji et al., Figure 1, Definition 4.5, Figure 2, and Theorem 4.7](https://arxiv.org/html/2111.08131) use a hidden subcube, send an ordered pair `(u,v)`, and receive two field values. The blueprint instead sends a seeded diagonal line and receives a polynomial. Its nontrivial cross-tests also have mass `2/9`, versus `1/3` in the cited game. A valid reduction must construct pair measurements by postprocessing, handle hidden subcubes and seed fibers while preserving projectivity and synchronicity, and prove the resulting value bound, approximately `rej_tensor ≤ 3/2 rej_ld + O(1/(mq))`. The claimed difference of only `2/(mq)` does not establish this.
./results/telemetry/registry-archive/prs/0001-docs-blueprint-qpbt-chapters-and-dependency-graph/reviews/4277943db23d2dde26e90908ede8041fb7902e36-code.md:41:**F2.** The same provider theorem requires its auxiliary parameter `K ≥ 12mt`. For the degree-`d` Reed–Solomon code, `t = d+1`, but line 164 takes `K=m³d`, which can be smaller than `12m(d+1)`. Taking `K ≥ max(m³d,12m(d+1))` and redoing the polynomial/exponential error absorption would repair this source-hypothesis omission.
./results/telemetry/registry-archive/prs/0001-docs-blueprint-qpbt-chapters-and-dependency-graph/reviews/4277943db23d2dde26e90908ede8041fb7902e36-code.md:43:**F3.** The QPBT mirror at [14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex](/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/.worktrees/issue-0002-qpbt-blueprint/references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:713), label `thm:linearity`, inserts `δ≤1`; [Natarajan–Vidick Theorem 10](https://arxiv.org/html/1610.03574v1) does not. Lines 233–241 then apply the restricted version separately to each `(x,z)`, although a small global average does not imply every conditional defect is at most one. Import the provider statement without this restriction, or supply an explicit good/bad-fiber saturation argument before averaging.
