\line_\\\\\\\\xpt =\\\\n  1079\\\\t            (v'_\\\\\\\\xpt,e_{i_\\\\\\\\xpt}),\\\\\\\\line_\\\\\\\\zpt = (v'_\\\\\\\\zpt, e_{i_\\\\\\\\zpt})$.\\\\n  1080\\\\t            \\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\n  1081\\\\t              \\\\\\\\item If $1 \\\\\\\\leq j \\\\\\\\leq m$, then set $i_\\\\\\\\xpt = j$ and\\\\n  1082\\\\t                choose $i_\\\\\\\\zpt$ to be uniformly random in $\\\\\\\\{1, \\\\\\\\dots,\\\\n  1083\\\\t                m\\\\\\\\}$.\\\\n  1084\\\\t              \\\\\\\\item If  $m+1 \\\\\\\\leq j \\\\\\\\leq 2m$, then set $i_\\\\\\\\xpt$ to be\\\\n  1085\\\\t                uniformly random in $\\\\\\\\{1, \\\\\\\\dots, m\\\\\\\\}$ and $i_\\\\\\\\zpt = j - m$.\\\\n  1086\\\\t              \\\\\\\\item If $2m + 1 \\\\\\\\leq j \\\\\\\\leq 2m+2$, then set $i_\\\\\\\\xpt$\\\\n  1087\\\\t                and $i_\\\\\\\\zpt$ to both be uniformly random in $\\\\\\\\{1, \\\\\\\\dots, m\\\\\\\\}$.\\\\n  1088\\\\t              \\\\\\\\item Let $v'_\\\\\\\\xpt$ be the canonical representative\\\\n  1089\\\\t                $L_{e_{i_\\\\\\\\xpt}}^\\\\\\\\lnf(v_\\\\\\\\xpt)$, and\\\\n  1090\\\\t                likewise let $v'_\\\\\\\\zpt$ be the canonical\\\\n  1091\\\\t                representative $L_{e_{i_\\\\\\\\zpt}}^\\\\\\\\lnf(v_{\\\\\\\\zpt})$ (see\\\\n  1092\\\\t                \\\\\\\\Cref{def:line-representative} for the definition of\\\\n  1093\\\\t                the canonical representative of a line). Note that\\\\n  1094\\\\t                $v_\\\\\\\\xpt$ lies on the line $\\\\\\\\line_\\\\\\\\xpt = (v'_\\\\\\\\xpt,\\\\n  1095\\\\t                e_{i_\\\\\\\\xpt})$, and similarly for $v_\\\\\\\\zpt$ and\\\\n  1096\\\\t                $\\\\\\\\line_{\\\\\\\\zpt} = (v'_\\\\\\\\zpt, e_{i_\\\\\\\\zpt})$.\\\\n  1097\\\\t              \\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\n  1098\\\\t              We claim that this construction satisfies Property~2 of the lemma. We show this first $(\\\\\\\\line_X,\\\\\\\\line_Z)$ and $u_X$, the other case being symmetric. If we are in case (a), then $u_X$ is a uniformly random point on the line $\\\\\\\\line_X=(v_X, e_j)$ and $\\\\\\\\line_Z=(v_Z,e_{i_Z})$, which is independent from $\\\\\\\\line_X$. Thus, the marginal distribution over $(\\\\\\\\line_X,u_X)$ conditioned on $j$ in this case is equal to $D_{\\\\\\\\ALine, j}$ and furthermore even conditioned on $(\\\\\\\\line_X,u_X)$, $\\\\\\\\line_Z$ is distributed according to $D_{\\\\\\\\ALine,i_Z}$ for a uniformly random $i_Z$. Cases (b) and (c) are analogous except that in this case even conditioned on $j$, $i_X$ is uniform. \\\\n  1099\\\\t      \\\\\\\\item Suppose $\\\\\\\\ell$ is a random diagonal line $(v, s, w)$,\\\\n  1100\\\\t        where $v = (v_\\\\\\\\xpt, v_\\\\\\\\zpt, \\\\\\\\alpha, \\\\\\\\beta)$, and\\\\n  1101\\\\t        where $w = (w_\\\\\\\\xpt, w_\\\\\\\\zpt, \\\\\\\\gamma, \\\\\\\\delta) $ is a direction vector whose first nonzero coordinate is\\\\n  1102\\\\t         $j = \\\\\\\\chi(s)$ (where $\\\\\\\\chi$ is defined in \\\\\\\\Cref{eq:chi-func}). We sample $\\\\\\\\line_\\\\\\\\xpt =  (v'_\\\\\\\\xpt, s_\\\\\\\\xpt,\\\\n  1103\\\\t        w'_\\\\\\\\xpt)$ and $\\\\\\\\line_\\\\\\\\zpt = (v'_\\\\\\\\zpt, s_\\\\\\\\zpt, w'_\\\\\\\\zpt)$ in the following\\\\n  1104\\\\t        manner:\\\\n  1105\\\\t        \\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\n  1106\\\\t          \\\\\\\\item If $1 \\\\\\\\leq j \\\\\\\\leq m$, then $w'_\\\\\\\\xpt = w_\\\\\\\\xpt$ and $w'_\\\\\\\\zpt = w_\\\\\\\\zpt$. We choose $s_\\\\\\\\xpt$ and $s_\\\\\\\\zpt$ such that $\\\\\\\\chi(s_\\\\\\\\xpt) = j$ and $\\\\\\\\chi(s_\\\\\\\\zpt) = 1$.\\\\n  1107\\\\t          \\\\\\\\item If $m+1 \\\\\\\\leq j \\\\\\\\leq 2m$, then $w'_\\\\\\\\xpt$ is a random\\\\n  1108\\\\t            diagonal line direction (and $s_{\\\\\\\\xpt}$ is chosen accordingly), and $w'_\\\\\\\\zpt = w_\\\\\\\\zpt$, with $s_\\\\\\\\zpt$ such that $\\\\\\\\chi(s_\\\\\\\\zpt) = j - m$.\\\\n  1109\\\\t          \\\\\\\\item If $2m+1 \\\\\\\\leq j \\\\\\\\leq 2m+2$ then both $w_\\\\\\\\xpt$, and\\\\n  1110\\\\t            $w_\\\\\\\\zpt$ are uniformly random, with $s_\\\\\\\\xpt$ and $s_\\\\\\\\zpt$ chosen accordingly.\\\\n  1111\\\\t          \\\\\\\\item Set $v'_\\\\\\\\xpt = L_{w'_{\\\\\\\\xpt}}^\\\\\\\\lnf(v_\\\\\\\\xpt)$ and $v'_\\\\\\\\zpt = L_{w'_{\\\\\\\\zpt}}^\\\\\\\\lnf(v_\\\\\\\\zpt)$.\\\\n  1112\\\\t          \\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\n  1113\\\\t          We claim that this construction satisfies Property~2 of the Lemma. We show this first $(\\\\\\\\line_X,\\\\\\\\line_Z)$ and $u_X$, the other case being symmetric.  If we are in case (a), then $u_X$ is a uniformly random point on the diagonal line $(v_X, s_X, w_X)$ which is equal to $\\\\\\\\line_X$. Thus, the marginal distribution over $(\\\\\\\\line_X, u_X)$ conditioned on $j$ in this case is equal to $D_{\\\\\\\\DLine, j}$. Furthermore, conditioned on $(\\\\\\\\line_X,u_X)$, $\\\\\\\\line_Z$ is distributed according to $D_{\\\\\\\\DLine,1}$. The other cases can be analyed analogously, with the only difference being how the indices $i,j$ of $\\\\\\\\line_X$ and $\\\\\\\\line_Z$ distributions are chosen. \\\\n  1114\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t    \\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\n  1115\\\\t\\\\tLet $D$ be the distribution over tuples $(\\\\\\\\line, \\\\\\\\line_X, \\\\\\\\line_Z)$ induced by this procedure. This distribution $D$ satisfies Property 1 in the lemma by  construction, and Property 2 as argued above.\\\\n  1116\\\\t    \\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n  1117\\\\t\\\\n  1118\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{proof}[Proof of \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:qld-4-13}]\\\\n  1119\\\\tWe construct the measurements $\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^\\\\\\\\line_f$\\\\n  1120\\\\tout of the lines measurements $T^{\\\\\\\\line_X, \\\\\\\\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}$\\\\n  1121\\\\tguaranteed by \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:qld-xz-lines}. To do so we first show how\\\\n  1122\\\\tto map any line-point pair $(\\\\\\\\line, u)$ in the expanded space\\\\n  1123\\\\t$\\\\\\\\F_q^{2m+2}$ to two pairs  $(\\\\\\\\line_X, u_X)$ and  $(\\\\\\\\line_Z, u_Z)$ in $\\\\\\\\F_q^m$. We will \\\\n  1124\\\\tdefine $\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^\\\\\\\\line$ in terms of $T^{\\\\\\\\line_X, \\\\\\\\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}$ and\\\\n  1125\\\\tshow that the resulting measurement is consistent with the combined\\\\n  1126\\\\tpoints measurement $\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\\\\\\\alpha, \\\\\\\\beta}$ from \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:qld-4-12}.\\\\n  1127\\\\t\\\\n  1128\\\\tTo define $\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^\\\\\\\\line$ we make use of the distribution $D$ from \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:qld-sublines}. Let $D_{|\\\\\\\\line}$ denote the distribution derived from $D$ by conditioning on the first element being $\\\\\\\\line$. Then for every line $\\\\\\\\line$ in $\\\\\\\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and function $f \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\deg_{(md+1)}(\\\\\\\\line)$, define\\\\n  1129\\\\t\\\\\\\\[ \\\\\\\\hat{Q}^\\\\\\\\line_f = \\\\\\\\E_{(\\\\\\\\line_X, \\\\\\\\line_Z) \\\\\\\\sim D_{|\\\\\\\\line}} \\\\\\\\sum_{\\\\\\\\substack{f_X \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\deg_{md}(\\\\\\\\line_X), f_Z \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\deg_{md}(\\\\\\\\line_Z):\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ f = \\\\\\\\combine_{f_X, f_Z}}} T^{\\\\\\\\line_X, \\\\\\\\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}\\\\\\\\;, \\\\\\\\]\\\\n  1130\\\\twhere $\\\\\\\\combine_{f_X, f_Z}$ is defined in~\\\\\\\\eqref{eq:combine-lines}. Note that $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^\\\\\\\\line_f\\\\\\\\}$ forms a valid POVM for every choice of line $\\\\\\\\line$, and moreover that $\\\\\\\\combine_{f_X, f_Z}$ is well-defined for the lines $\\\\\\\\line, \\\\\\\\line_X, \\\\\\\\line_Z$ because of Property 2 of \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:qld-sublines}. \\\\n  1131\\\\t\\\\n  1132\\\\tObserve that by Property~3 of \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:qld-sublines}, whenever $\\\\\\\\line$ is axis-parallel, $\\\\\\\\line_X$ and $\\\\\\\\line_Z$ are axis-parallel also. Thus, in this case, by \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:qld-xz-lines} it holds that the outcomes $f_X$ and $f_Z$ both have degree $d$. By definition of $\\\\\\\\combine_{f_X, f_Z}$ it holds that the outcome $f$ of $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^\\\\\\\\line_f\\\\\\\\}$ has degree $d$ as well.\\\\n  1133\\\\t\\\\n  1134\\\\tWe argue the following bound:\\\\n  1135\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{align}\\\\n  1136\\\\t  \\\\\\\\E_{(\\\\\\\\line, \\\\\\\\line_X, \\\\\\\\line_Z) \\\\\\\\sim D} \\\\\\\\E_{(x,z,\\\\\\\\alpha, \\\\\\\\beta) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\line} &\\\\\\\\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\hat{\\\\\\\\psi}} T^{\\\\\\\\line_X, \\\\\\\\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\\\\\\\ot (I - \\\\\\\\hat{Q}^{x, z }_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}) \\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\hat{\\\\\\\\psi}}  \\\\\\\\,=\\\\\\\\, O\\\\\\\\big( m \\\\\\\\cdot (\\\\\\\\eps^{1/4} + \\\\\\\\delta_P^{1/4} + \\\\\\\\delta_Q^{1/4} + \\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\Line}^{1/2})\\\\\\\\big)\\\\\\\\;. \\\\\\\\label{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}\\\\n  1137\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{align}\\\\n  1138\\\\tThis bound will follow from chaining the following three claims. \\\\n  1139\\\\t\\\\n  1140\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{claim}\\\\\\\\label{claim:17-1}\\\\n  1141\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{align}\\\\n  1142\\\\t  \\\\\\\\E_{(\\\\\\\\line, \\\\\\\\line_X, \\\\\\\\line_Z) \\\\\\\\sim D} \\\\\\\\E_{(x,z,\\\\\\\\alpha, \\\\\\\\beta) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\line} &\\\\\\\\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\hat{\\\\\\\\psi}} T^{\\\\\\\\line_X, \\\\\\\\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\hat{Q}^{x, z }_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)} \\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\hat{\\\\\\\\psi}}  \\\\\\\\notag\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  1270\\\\t  (\\\\\\\\hat{S}_{[\\\\\\\\eval_{u}(\\\\\\\\cdot_{W}) = a]})_{\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\alice'} &\\\\\\\\simeq_{\\\\\\\\delta_S} (\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\alice''} \\\\\\\\label{eq:qld-s-point-con-alice} \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ \\\\n  1271\\\\t    (\\\\\\\\hat{S}_{[\\\\\\\\eval_{u}(\\\\\\\\cdot_{W}) = a]})_{\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\bob'} &\\\\\\\\simeq_{\\\\\\\\delta_S} (\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\bob''} \\\\\\\\label{eq:qld-s-point-con-bob},\\\\n  1272\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{align}\\\\n  1273\\\\twhere the notation $\\\\\\\\eval_{u}(\\\\\\\\cdot_W)$ means the evaluation of the first outcome $g_X$ of $\\\\\\\\hat{S}$ if $W = X$, or the second outcome $g_Z$ if $W = Z$, at the point $u$.\\\\n  1274\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\n  1275\\\\t\\\\n  1276\\\\t\\\\n  1277\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\n  1278\\\\t\\\\tLemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} establishes the existence of a strategy $\\\\\\\\strategy_\\\\\\\\ld = (\\\\\\\\hat{\\\\\\\\psi}, \\\\\\\\{\\\\n  1279\\\\t        \\\\\\\\hat{Q}^\\\\\\\\line_f \\\\\\\\} \\\\\\\\cup \\\\\\\\{ \\\\\\\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\\\\\\\alpha,\\\\\\\\beta}_a \\\\\\\\} )$\\\\n  1280\\\\t        for the classical low-degree test $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\ld_\\\\\\\\ldparams$ for\\\\n  1281\\\\t        $\\\\\\\\ldparams = (q,2m + 2,d, 1)$, with value $1 -\\\\n  1282\\\\t        \\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\combine}$. This strategy is not necessarily projective because the measurement $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hat{Q}^{\\\\\\\\line}_f\\\\\\\\}$ is not guaranteed to be; however, we may projectivize it by applying Naimark's theorem to this measurement. This preserves the consistency ($\\\\\\\\simeq$) relation obtained as the conclusion of \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:qld-4-13}. Theorem~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} applied to this projectivized strategy implies the\\\\n  1283\\\\t        existence of a POVM $\\\\\\\\{ \\\\\\\\hat{S}_g \\\\\\\\}$ with\\\\n  1284\\\\t        outcomes $g \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\ideg_{d,2m+2}(\\\\\\\\F_q)$ that is\\\\n  1285\\\\t        $\\\\\\\\delta_\\\\\\\\ld$-self-consistent and consistent with the\\\\n  1286\\\\t        ``points'' measurements $\\\\\\\\{ \\\\\\\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\\\\\\\alpha,\\\\\\\\beta}_a\\\\\\\\}$,\\\\n  1287\\\\t        where $\\\\\\\\delta_\\\\\\\\ld = a(md)^a(\\\\\\\\delta_\\\\\\\\combine^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ for some universal constants $a > 1, 0 < b < 1$ is given\\\\n  1288\\\\t        by Theorem~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}. We may apply Naimark's theorem once again to $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hat{S}_g\\\\\\\\}$ to obtain a projective measurement with the same consistency guarantees, and henceforth, we will use $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hat{S}_g\\\\\\\\}$ to refer to this projective measurement.\\\\n  1289\\\\t\\\\t\\\\n  1290\\\\t\\\\tWe now argue that with high probability, the polynomial $g$ returned by the measurement $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hat{S}_g \\\\\\\\}$ has the form $g(x,z,\\\\\\\\alpha,\\\\\\\\beta) = \\\\\\\\alpha g_\\\\\\\\xpt(x) + \\\\\\\\beta g_\\\\\\\\zpt (z)$ for polynomials $g_\\\\\\\\xpt, g_\\\\\\\\zpt \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\ideg_{d,m}(\\\\\\\\F_q)$. Let $\\\\\\\\mathcal{G}$ denote the set of such polynomials. On average over uniformly random $(x,z,\\\\\\\\alpha,\\\\\\\\beta)$, \\\\n  1291\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{align}\\\\n  1292\\\\t          (\\\\\\\\hat{S}_g)_{\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\alice'} &= (\\\\\\\\hat{S}_g)^2_{\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\alice'} \\\\\\\\notag \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n  1858\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n  1859\\\\t\\\\tThis concludes the proof of the second item of the lemma.\\\\n  1860\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n  1861\\\\t\\\\n  1862\\\\tWe now prove \\\\\\\\Cref{thm:pauli-appendix}. \\\\n  1863\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{proof}[Proof of \\\\\\\\Cref{thm:pauli-appendix}]\\\\n  1864\\\\t\\\\tLet $\\\\\\\\strategy = (\\\\\\\\psi,M)$ be a projective strategy for $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\pauli_\\\\\\\\qldparams$ that succeeds with probability at least $1 - \\\\\\\\eps$, where $\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\psi}$ is a bipartite state on registers $\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\bob$. As mentioned at the beginning of \\\\\\\\Cref{sec:commutation}, we assume that the state $\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\psi}$ is padded with sufficiently many ancilla $\\\\\\\\ket{0}$ qubits. Let $\\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\alice$ be the following isometry mapping the register $\\\\\\\\alice$ to the registers $\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\alice' \\\\\\\\alice''$ where $\\\\\\\\alice'$ and $\\\\\\\\alice''$ are isomorphic to $(\\\\\\\\C^q)^{\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\nqubits}$:\\\\n  1865\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n  1866\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\alice: \\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\theta}_\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\mapsto V_\\\\\\\\alice (\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\theta}_\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\otimes (\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\nqubits})_{\\\\\\\\alice' \\\\\\\\alice''})\\\\n  1867\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n  1868\\\\t\\\\twhere $V_\\\\\\\\alice$ is the unitary acting on $\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\alice' \\\\\\\\alice''$ from \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:qld-unitary}. Define the isometry $\\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\bob$ analogously. \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:qld-unitary} then implies that there exists a state $\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\aux}$ on registers $\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\alice' \\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\bob'$ such that\\\\n  1869\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n  1870\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\left \\\\\\\\| \\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\psi} - \\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\aux} \\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\nqubits} \\\\\\\\right \\\\\\\\|^2 \\\\\\\\leq \\\\\\\\delta_\\\\\\\\qld\\\\n  1871\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n  1872\\\\t\\\\tand for $W \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{X,Z\\\\\\\\}$,\\\\n  1873\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n  1874\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\, (M^{(\\\\\\\\Pauli,W)}_h)_\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\alice^\\\\\\\\dagger \\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\delta_\\\\\\\\qld} (\\\\\\\\tau^W_h)_{\\\\\\\\alice''} \\\\\\\\qquad \\\\\\\\text{and} \\\\\\\\qquad \\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\, (M^{(\\\\\\\\Pauli,W)}_h)_\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\phi_\\\\\\\\bob^\\\\\\\\dagger \\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\delta_\\\\\\\\qld} (\\\\\\\\tau^W_h)_{\\\\\\\\bob''}\\\\n  1875\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n  1876\\\\t\\\\twhere the $\\\\\\\\approx$ statement holds with respect to the state $\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\aux} \\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\otimes \\\\\\\\nqubits}$. This shows that $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\pauli_\\\\\\\\qldparams$ is a self-test for the strategy $\\\\\\\\strategy^\\\\\\\\pauli$ with robustness $\\\\\\\\delta_\\\\\\\\qld(\\\\\\\\eps,m,d,q)$. We conclude the proof by making the identification of registers $\\\\\\\\alice \\\\\\\\alice'$ with $\\\\\\\\alice'$ and $\\\\\\\\bob \\\\\\\\bob'$ with $\\\\\\\\bob'$ to be consistent with the statement of \\\\\\\\Cref{thm:pauli-appendix}. We also note that, when unpacking the dependence on parameters $\\\\\\\\eps, m, d, q$, the function $\\\\\\\\delta_\\\\\\\\qld(\\\\\\\\eps,m,d,q) = O(\\\\\\\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$ has the form $a(md)^a(\\\\\\\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ for some universal constants $a > 1, 0 < b < 1$, as desired.\\\\n  1877\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n  1878\\\\t \\\\n  1879\\\\t\\\\n  1880\\\\t \\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:327:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_168\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/04_preliminaries.tex | sed -n '1,12p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex | sed -n '28,40p;100,120p;225,275p;325,390p;895,970p'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"     1\\\\t\\\\\\\\section{Preliminaries}\\\\n     2\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{sec:prelim}\\\\n     3\\\\t\\\\n     4\\\\t\\\\\\\\paragraph{Notation.}\\\\n     5\\\\tWe use $\\\\\\\\Sigma$ to denote a finite alphabet.\\\\n     6\\\\t$\\\\\\\\N$ is the set of positive integers.\\\\n     7\\\\tFor $w\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\{0,1\\\\\\\\}$, $\\\\\\\\overline{w}$ denotes $1-w$.\\\\n     8\\\\tFor $w\\\\\\\\in\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\alice,\\\\\\\\bob\\\\\\\\}$, $\\\\\\\\overline{w}=\\\\\\\\bob$ if $w=\\\\\\\\alice$ and\\\\n     9\\\\t$\\\\\\\\overline{w}=\\\\\\\\alice$ otherwise.\\\\n    10\\\\t(For notational convenience we often implicitly make the identifications\\\\n    11\\\\t$1\\\\\\\\leftrightarrow \\\\\\\\alice$ and $2\\\\\\\\leftrightarrow \\\\\\\\bob$.)\\\\n    12\\\\tWe use $\\\\\\\\F$ to denote a finite field.\\\\n    28\\\\t\\\\n    29\\\\t\\\\n    30\\\\t\\\\\\\\subsubsection{The game}\\\\n    31\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{sec:ld-game}\\\\n    32\\\\t\\\\n    33\\\\tThe game $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\ld$ is parametrized by a tuple $\\\\\\\\ldparams = (q, m, d, \\\\\\\\ldc)$\\\\n    34\\\\twhere $m, d, \\\\\\\\ldc \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\N$ are integers, $q \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\N$ is an admissible field size,\\\\n    35\\\\tand $m$ divides $q$.\\\\n    36\\\\tWe sometimes write $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\ld_\\\\\\\\ldparams$ to emphasize the dependence of the\\\\n    37\\\\tclassical low-degree test on the parameter tuple $\\\\\\\\ldparams$.\\\\n    38\\\\t\\\\n    39\\\\tWe first provide a high-level description of the game for $k=1$.\\\\n    40\\\\tIt is based on the low-individual degree variant of the multilinearity test\\\\n   100\\\\t\\\\n   101\\\\t\\\\n   102\\\\t\\\\\\\\paragraph{Axis-parallel lines and diagonal lines.}\\\\n   103\\\\tBefore presenting a formal description of the game $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\ld$, we first define\\\\n   104\\\\twhat we mean by lines, and their properties.\\\\n   105\\\\t\\\\n   106\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Lines]\\\\n   107\\\\t  \\\\\\\\label{def:line}\\\\n   108\\\\t\\\\tA \\\\\\\\emph{line} $\\\\\\\\line$ in $\\\\\\\\F^m_q$ specified by a pair $(u,v) \\\\\\\\in (\\\\\\\\F_q^m)^2$\\\\n   109\\\\t  is the subset\\\\n   110\\\\t  \\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\n   111\\\\t    \\\\\\\\label{eq:line-set}\\\\n   112\\\\t    \\\\\\\\line(u,v) = \\\\\\\\bigl\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\, u + tv : t \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\bigr\\\\\\\\} \\\\\\\\subseteq \\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\;.\\\\n   113\\\\t  \\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\n   114\\\\t  We call the vector $v$ a \\\\\\\\emph{direction} of line\\\\n   115\\\\t  $\\\\\\\\line(u,v)$.\\\\\\\\footnote{For convenience we explicitly allow\\\\n   116\\\\t    $v=0$, in which case the line is reduced to a singleton.}\\\\n   117\\\\t  \\\\n   118\\\\t  A line $\\\\\\\\line(u,v)$ is \\\\\\\\emph{axis-parallel} if there is a coordinate $i \\\\\\\\in\\\\n   119\\\\t  \\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\}$ for which $v$ is equal to $e_i$, the $i$-th elementary basis\\\\n   120\\\\t  vector in $\\\\\\\\F_q^m$.\\\\n   225\\\\tfunctions $\\\\\\\\{L^\\\\\\\\lnf_{e_i} \\\\\\\\}$ acting on $V_\\\\\\\\xpt$, indexed by $i \\\\\\\\in\\\\n   226\\\\t\\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\}$.\\\\n   227\\\\t(See \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:cl-concat} for definition of CL function concatenation.)\\\\n   228\\\\tThus $L_\\\\\\\\ALine$ is a $2$-level CL function.\\\\n   229\\\\t\\\\n   230\\\\tDefine $L_\\\\\\\\DLine$ to be the following $3$-level CL function: for all $(u,s,v) \\\\\\\\in\\\\n   231\\\\tV$,\\\\n   232\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\n   233\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{eq:cl-dlnf}\\\\n   234\\\\t\\\\tL_\\\\\\\\DLine(u,s,v) = (L^\\\\\\\\lnf_{\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)}(u),s,\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v))\\\\n   235\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{equation}\\\\n   236\\\\twhere $i = \\\\\\\\chi(s)$.\\\\n   237\\\\tThe function $L_\\\\\\\\DLine$ is the concatenation of the following CL functions (again, see \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:cl-concat} for definition of CL function concatenation): (i) the identity function on $V_{\\\\\\\\coord}$ (which is a $1$-level CL function); (ii) for every $i \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\}$ the $1$-level CL function on $V_{\\\\\\\\dir{}}$ that maps $v \\\\\\\\mapsto \\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)$ (i.e.~it zeroes out the first $i-1$ coordinates of $v$), and (iii) for every pair $(s,v') \\\\\\\\in V_\\\\\\\\coord \\\\\\\\oplus V_{\\\\\\\\dir{}}$, the $1$-level CL function $L^\\\\\\\\lnf_{v'}$ acting on $V_\\\\\\\\xpt$. Thus $L_\\\\\\\\DLine$ is a $3$-level CL function.\\\\n   238\\\\t\\\\n   239\\\\t\\\\n   240\\\\tThe following two lemmas establish that the CL functions\\\\n   241\\\\t$L_\\\\\\\\Point,L_\\\\\\\\ALine,L_\\\\\\\\DLine$ give rise to the axis-parallel line-versus-point and\\\\n   242\\\\tdiagonal line-versus-point distributions.\\\\n   243\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}\\\\n   244\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{lem:alnf}\\\\n   245\\\\t\\\\tConsider the pair $(\\\\\\\\line, u)$ sampled in the following way: first, sample the\\\\n   246\\\\t  tuple $((u_0,s,0),(u,0,0))$ from the CL distribution $\\\\\\\\mu_{L_\\\\\\\\ALine,L_\\\\\\\\Point}$,\\\\n   247\\\\t  and let $\\\\\\\\line = \\\\\\\\line(u_0,e_i)$ where $i = \\\\\\\\chi(s)$.\\\\n   248\\\\t  Then $u$ is a uniformly random point in $\\\\\\\\F_q^m$ and $\\\\\\\\line$ is a line that\\\\n   249\\\\t  goes through $u$ and is parallel to a uniformly random axis $i \\\\\\\\in\\\\n   250\\\\t  \\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\}$.\\\\n   251\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\n   252\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\n   253\\\\t\\\\tSince $s$ is uniformly random in $\\\\\\\\F_q$ and $m$ divides $q$ by assumption, we\\\\n   254\\\\t  have that $i$ is uniformly random between $\\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\ldots,m\\\\\\\\}$ and therefore\\\\n   255\\\\t  $(u_0,s)$ specifies a uniformly random axis-parallel line $\\\\\\\\line(u_0,e_i)$.\\\\n   256\\\\t  Since $u_0 = L^{\\\\\\\\lnf}_{e_i}(u)$, we have that $u \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\line(u_0,e_i)$.\\\\n   257\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n   258\\\\t\\\\n   259\\\\t\\\\n   260\\\\t\\\\n   261\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}\\\\n   262\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{lem:dlnf}\\\\n   263\\\\t\\\\tConsider the pair $(\\\\\\\\line,u)$ sampled in the following way: first, sample the\\\\n   264\\\\t  tuple $((u_0,s,v),(u,0,0))$ from the CL distribution $\\\\\\\\mu_{L_\\\\\\\\DLine,L_\\\\\\\\Point}$,\\\\n   265\\\\t  and let $\\\\\\\\line = \\\\\\\\line(u_0,v)$ where $i = \\\\\\\\chi(s)$.\\\\n   266\\\\t  Then $u$ is a uniformly random point in $\\\\\\\\F_q^m$ and $\\\\\\\\line$ is a random\\\\n   267\\\\t  diagonal line that goes through $u$ and shares the first $i-1$ coordinates\\\\n   268\\\\t  with $u$, for uniformly random $i$.\\\\n   269\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\n   270\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\n   271\\\\t\\\\tThe proof of this follows identically to \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:alnf}.\\\\n   272\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n   273\\\\t\\\\n   274\\\\tFor later convenience we make the following definition. \\\\n   275\\\\t\\\\n   325\\\\tcontent $x$, a bit string of length $m \\\\\\\\log q$, should be interpreted by the\\\\n   326\\\\tdecision procedure and the players as an element of the vector space $\\\\\\\\F_q^m$,\\\\n   327\\\\tas indicated in Section~\\\\\\\\ref{sec:ff-representations}.\\\\n   328\\\\tSimilarly the answer to a question with type $\\\\\\\\Point$ is expected to be a bit\\\\n   329\\\\tstring of length $\\\\\\\\ldc \\\\\\\\log q$, and is interpreted as an element of $\\\\\\\\F_q^\\\\\\\\ldc$.\\\\n   330\\\\tFor questions with type $\\\\\\\\ALine$, the question content is a $(m \\\\\\\\log q + \\\\\\\\log\\\\n   331\\\\tq)$-bit string, which is parsed as a pair $(u_0, s) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\F_q$,\\\\n   332\\\\twhich in turn can be interpreted as a specification for an axis-parallel line\\\\n   333\\\\t$\\\\\\\\line(u_0,e_i)$ in $\\\\\\\\F_q^m$ where $i = \\\\\\\\chi(s)$, as described in\\\\n   334\\\\t\\\\\\\\Cref{lem:alnf}.\\\\n   335\\\\tThe answer is interpreted as the description of $\\\\\\\\ldc$ degree-$d$ univariate\\\\n   336\\\\tpolynomials defined on the line $\\\\\\\\line(u_0,e_i)$, each polynomial given by a\\\\n   337\\\\tlist of $d+1$ coefficients.\\\\n   338\\\\tFor questions with type $\\\\\\\\DLine$, the question content is a $(2m \\\\\\\\log q + \\\\\\\\log\\\\n   339\\\\tq)$-bit string, which is parsed as a triple $(u_0, s, v) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\F_q\\\\n   340\\\\t\\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\F_q^m$ that specifies a diagonal line $\\\\\\\\line(u_0,v')$ where $v' =\\\\n   341\\\\t\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)$ as described in \\\\\\\\Cref{lem:dlnf}.\\\\n   342\\\\tThe answer is interpreted as the description of $\\\\\\\\ldc$ degree-$md$ univariate\\\\n   343\\\\tpolynomials defined on the line $\\\\\\\\line(u_0,v')$.\\\\n   344\\\\tIf the answers returned by the players do not fit this format the decision\\\\n   345\\\\tprocedure rejects.\\\\n   346\\\\t\\\\n   347\\\\t\\\\n   348\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{figure}[!htbp]\\\\n   349\\\\t  \\\\\\\\centering\\\\n   350\\\\t  \\\\\\\\begin{gamespec}\\\\n   351\\\\t    \\\\\\\\setlength{\\\\\\\\tabcolsep}{1em}\\\\n   352\\\\t    \\\\\\\\begin{tabularx}{\\\\\\\\textwidth}{ l   l   X   }\\\\n   353\\\\t      \\\\\\\\toprule\\\\n   354\\\\t      Type & Question Content & Answer Format \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   355\\\\t      \\\\\\\\midrule\\\\n   356\\\\t      $\\\\\\\\Point$ & $ u \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m$ & Element of $\\\\\\\\F_q^\\\\\\\\ldc$  \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   357\\\\t      $\\\\\\\\ALine$ & $ (u_0, s) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\F_q$ &\\\\n   358\\\\t      $\\\\\\\\ldc$ degree-$d$ polynomials $f_j: \\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\F_q$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   359\\\\t      $\\\\\\\\DLine$ & $(u_0, s, v) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\F_q^m$ & \\\\n   360\\\\t      $\\\\\\\\ldc$ degree-$md$ polynomials $f_j: \\\\\\\\F_q \\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\F_q$\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\n   361\\\\t      \\\\\\\\bottomrule\\\\n   362\\\\t    \\\\\\\\end{tabularx}\\\\n   363\\\\t\\\\n   364\\\\t    \\\\\\\\vspace{1em}\\\\n   365\\\\t\\\\n   366\\\\t    Input to $\\\\\\\\decider^\\\\\\\\ld$: $(\\\\\\\\tvar_\\\\\\\\alice,x_\\\\\\\\alice, \\\\\\\\tvar_\\\\\\\\bob, x_\\\\\\\\bob,\\\\n   367\\\\t    a_\\\\\\\\alice, a_\\\\\\\\bob)$.\\\\n   368\\\\t    In all cases where no action is indicated, accept.\\\\n   369\\\\t    For $w \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\AB$,\\\\n   370\\\\t\\\\n   371\\\\t    \\\\\\\\begin{enumerate}\\\\n   372\\\\t      \\\\\\\\setlength\\\\\\\\itemsep{1pt}\\\\n   373\\\\t    \\\\\\\\item (\\\\\\\\textbf{Consistency test}) If $\\\\\\\\tvar_\\\\\\\\alice = \\\\\\\\tvar_\\\\\\\\bob$, accept iff\\\\n   374\\\\t      $a_\\\\\\\\alice = a_\\\\\\\\bob$.\\\\n   375\\\\t    \\\\\\\\item (\\\\\\\\textbf{Axis-parallel line-versus-point test}) If $\\\\\\\\tvar_w = \\\\\\\\ALine$\\\\n   376\\\\t      and $\\\\\\\\tvar_{\\\\\\\\overline{w}} = \\\\\\\\Point$, accept iff $f_j(t) =\\\\n   377\\\\t      (a_{\\\\\\\\overline{w}})_j$ for all $j \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\ldots,\\\\\\\\ldc\\\\\\\\}$ where $t \\\\\\\\in\\\\n   378\\\\t      \\\\\\\\F_q$ is such that $x_{\\\\\\\\overline{w}} = u_0 + te_i$ where $i = \\\\\\\\chi(s)$.\\\\n   379\\\\t      \\\\n   380\\\\t    \\\\\\\\item (\\\\\\\\textbf{Diagonal line-versus-point test}) If $\\\\\\\\tvar_w = \\\\\\\\DLine$ and\\\\n   381\\\\t      $\\\\\\\\tvar_{\\\\\\\\overline{w}} = \\\\\\\\Point$, accept iff $f_j(t) =\\\\n   382\\\\t      (a_{\\\\\\\\overline{w}})_j$ for all $j \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{1,2,\\\\\\\\ldots,\\\\\\\\ldc\\\\\\\\}$ where $t \\\\\\\\in\\\\n   383\\\\t      \\\\\\\\F_q$ is such that $x_{\\\\\\\\overline{w}} = u_0 + tv'$, where $v' =\\\\n   384\\\\t      \\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)$ with $i = \\\\\\\\chi(s)$.\\\\n   385\\\\t    \\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\n   386\\\\t  \\\\\\\\end{gamespec}\\\\n   387\\\\t  \\\\\\\\caption{The decision procedure $\\\\\\\\decider^\\\\\\\\ld$ for the simultaneous low-degree\\\\n   388\\\\t    test, parameterized by $\\\\\\\\ldparams = (q,m,d,\\\\\\\\ldc)$.\\\\n   389\\\\t    The function $\\\\\\\\chi(s)$ is defined in \\\\\\\\Cref{eq:chi-func}, and $\\\\\\\\pi_{i-1}(v)$\\\\n   390\\\\t    zeroes out the first $i-1$ coordinates of $v$.}\\\\n   895\\\\tSection~\\\\\\\\ref{sec:ld-verifier}.\\\\n   896\\\\tIn Section~\\\\\\\\ref{sec:qld-complexity} we exhibit canonical parameters for the\\\\n   897\\\\tPauli basis test and give bounds on the time complexity of executing the test.\\\\n   898\\\\t\\\\n   899\\\\t\\\\\\\\subsubsection{The game}\\\\n   900\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{sec:qld-game}\\\\n   901\\\\t\\\\n   902\\\\tWe start by discussing parameter settings.\\\\n   903\\\\tThe game $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\pauli$ is parametrized by a tuple\\\\n   904\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\\\\\qldparams = (q,m,d)\\\\\\\\;,\\\\\\\\] where $q,m,d \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\N$ are integers. \\\\n   905\\\\tWe sometimes write $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\pauli_\\\\\\\\qldparams$ to emphasize the dependence of the\\\\n   906\\\\tPauli basis test on the parameters.\\\\n   907\\\\t\\\\n   908\\\\tInformally, the test is meant to certify that the players share a state of the\\\\n   909\\\\tform $\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\EPR_q}^{\\\\\\\\otimes M}$, where $M=2^m$.\\\\n   910\\\\tIts question set includes questions that are axis-parallel/diagonal lines and points in $\\\\\\\\F_q^m$,\\\\n   911\\\\twhich are meant to correspond to questions in the classical low-degree test, and questions\\\\n   912\\\\tof the form $(\\\\\\\\Pauli, W)$, for $W \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{X, Z\\\\\\\\}$.\\\\n   913\\\\tUpon receipt of a question of the latter form, the players are expected to\\\\n   914\\\\tperform the POVM $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\tau_h^W\\\\\\\\}_{h \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^{M}}$ and report the outcome $h$ as\\\\n   915\\\\ttheir answer.\\\\n   916\\\\t\\\\n   917\\\\tThe key idea behind the test\\\\n   918\\\\tis for the provers to encode their outcomes using the \\\\\\\\emph{low-degree encoding} from Section~\\\\\\\\ref{sec:ld-encoding}.\\\\n   919\\\\tGiven an outcome $h \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^M$, the low-degree encoding of~$h$ is the polynomial $g_h:\\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\rightarrow \\\\\\\\F_q$.\\\\n   920\\\\tRather than asking the provers to always return the entire string~$h$,\\\\n   921\\\\tmany of the subtests in the game $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\pauli$ will ``probe'' limited information about~$h$ as follows:\\\\n   922\\\\tthe verifier provides the provers with a string $u_W \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^m$,\\\\n   923\\\\tand using this they are expected to evaluate $g_h(u_W) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q$ and return it to the verifier.\\\\n   924\\\\tFor a fixed value of~$W$, then, the prover's responses to these ``probes'' should be consistent with some low-degree polynomial in the input~$u_W$.\\\\n   925\\\\tTo check this, the verifier can perform the classical low-degree test from Section~\\\\\\\\ref{sec:ld-verifier}.\\\\n   926\\\\tThis ensures that the prover's responses for the $W = X$ basis are consistent with each other,\\\\n   927\\\\tas well as their responses for the $W = Z$ basis, although it does not test consistency between the $X$ and $Z$ responses.\\\\n   928\\\\t\\\\n   929\\\\tThe next set of subtests the verifier performs ensure that the provers' $X$ and $Z$ measurements are consistent with each other.\\\\n   930\\\\tOn the $X$ side, consider the measurement in which the prover performs the $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\tau_h^X\\\\\\\\}$ POVM,\\\\n   931\\\\treceives outcome $h_X$, and outputs $g_{h_X}(u_X) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q$.\\\\n   932\\\\tFor technical reasons that will become clear shortly,\\\\n   933\\\\tit will be convenient for the prover's measurement to have two outcomes rather than~$q$ outcomes.\\\\n   934\\\\tTo accomplish this, the verifier also provides the prover with an element $r_X \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q$;\\\\n   935\\\\tgiven this, the prover should output not $g_{h_X}(u_X) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q$ but $\\\\\\\\tr(g_{h_X}(u_X) \\\\\\\\cdot r_X)$, which is an element of $\\\\\\\\F_2$.\\\\n   936\\\\tSimilarly, on the $Z$ side, suppose the prover is provided $r_Z \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q$,\\\\n   937\\\\tperforms $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\tau_h^Z\\\\\\\\}$ to receive $h_Z$, and outputs $\\\\\\\\tr(g_{h_Z}(u_Z) \\\\\\\\cdot r_Z) \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_2$.\\\\n   938\\\\tTogether, these two are a pair of two-outcome measurements, one in the~$X$ basis and one in the~$Z$ basis,\\\\n   939\\\\tand as it turns out, this pair of measurements either commutes with each other or anticommutes with each other\\\\n   940\\\\t(this is why we modified the measurements to have only two outcomes).\\\\n   941\\\\tThe key quantity to determine which of these is the case is the following complicated-looking expression:\\\\n   942\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{equation*}\\\\n   943\\\\t\\\\\\\\gamma = \\\\\\\\tr \\\\\\\\bigl( (\\\\\\\\ind_{m}(u_\\\\\\\\xpt) \\\\\\\\cdot r_\\\\\\\\xpt) \\\\\\\\cdot\\\\n   944\\\\t      (\\\\\\\\ind_{m}(u_\\\\\\\\zpt) \\\\\\\\cdot r_\\\\\\\\zpt) \\\\\\\\bigr)\\\\\\\\;.\\\\n   945\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{equation*}\\\\n   946\\\\tIf $\\\\\\\\gamma = 0$, then these two measurements commute, and if $\\\\\\\\gamma = 1$, then these two measurements anticommute\\\\n   947\\\\t(this fact is established in the proof of Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:pauli-completeness} below).\\\\n   948\\\\tIn the $\\\\\\\\gamma = 0$ case, the verifier can check whether these two measurements commute by performing the ``commutation check'',\\\\n   949\\\\twhich asks the provers to simultaneously measure both $\\\\\\\\tr(g_{h_X}(u_X) \\\\\\\\cdot r_X)$ and $\\\\\\\\tr(g_{h_Z}(u_Z) \\\\\\\\cdot r_Z)$ and report their values.\\\\n   950\\\\tIn the $\\\\\\\\gamma = 1$ case, on the other hand, the measurements anticommute, which implies that they cannot simultaneously be measured.\\\\n   951\\\\tIn spite of this, the verifier can still check that the measurements anticommute by using the Magic Square game from Section~\\\\\\\\ref{sec:ms} above.\\\\n   952\\\\tAs was shown in Theorem~\\\\\\\\ref{thm:ms-from-ac}, any pair of anticommuting measurements\\\\n   953\\\\tcan be used to form a perfect strategy for the Magic Square game\\\\n   954\\\\t(and indeed the reverse is true as well: any perfect strategy for the Magic Square game entails a pair of anticommuting measurements).\\\\n   955\\\\t\\\\n   956\\\\tTogether, these give the main subtests for the game $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\pauli$.\\\\n   957\\\\t\\\\n   958\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Admissible parameters]\\\\\\\\label{def:admissible}\\\\n   959\\\\t  We say that the tuple $\\\\\\\\qldparams=(q, m, d)$ is \\\\\\\\emph{admissible} if $q$ is\\\\n   960\\\\t  an admissible field size (Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:admissible-size}) and $m | q$.\\\\n   961\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\n   962\\\\t\\\\n   963\\\\t\\\\n   964\\\\t\\\\\\\\paragraph{Question distribution.}\\\\n   965\\\\tWe now describe the question distribution of the game $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\pauli$, and show\\\\n   966\\\\tthat it is a (typed) CL distribution.\\\\n   967\\\\tThe question types in the game $\\\\\\\\game^\\\\\\\\pauli$ are\\\\n   968\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{equation}\\\\n   969\\\\t  \\\\\\\\label{eq:pauli-type}\\\\n   970\\\\t  \\\\\\\\type^\\\\\\\\pauli= \\\\\\\\bigl( \\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\Point, \\\\\\\\ALine, \\\\\\\\DLine, \\\\\\\\Pauli, \\\\\\\\Pair \\\\\\\\} \\\\\\\\times \\\\\\\\{X,Z\\\\\\\\} \\\\\\\\bigr)\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:331:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_170\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex | sed -n '38,60p;255,277p;750,780p;795,875p'\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"    38\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{def:ideg-deg-polynomials}\\\\n    39\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:line}\\\\n    40\\\\t\\\\tGiven integers $d, m \\\\\\\\geq 0$ and a prime power $q$, denote by\\\\n    41\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\\\\\\\F_q)$ the set of polynomials in $m$ variables over\\\\n    42\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\F_q$ with \\\\\\\\emph{individual} degree at most $d$ in each variable. When\\\\n    43\\\\t\\\\tthe field is clear from context we write $\\\\\\\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}$, and when\\\\n    44\\\\t\\\\tthe number of variables is also clear from context, $\\\\\\\\mathrm{ideg}_d$. We\\\\n    45\\\\t\\\\tdenote by $\\\\\\\\deg_d$ the set of polynomials with \\\\\\\\emph{total} degree at\\\\n    46\\\\t\\\\tmost $d$.\\\\n    47\\\\t\\\\t% The following usage is introduced only implicitly in the source, in the\\\\n    48\\\\t\\\\t% statement of the lemma labelled lem:qld-comm-line-cons.\\\\n    49\\\\t\\\\tFor a line $\\\\\\\\ell \\\\\\\\subseteq \\\\\\\\F_q^m$ (Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:line}) we write\\\\n    50\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\deg_{md}(\\\\\\\\ell)$ for the set of functions $\\\\\\\\ell \\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\F_q$ given by a\\\\n    51\\\\t\\\\tunivariate polynomial of degree at most $md$ in a parameterization of\\\\n    52\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\ell$.\\\\n    53\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{definition}\\\\n    54\\\\t\\\\n    55\\\\tThroughout this chapter $q = 2^k$ with $k$ odd is an admissible field size\\\\n    56\\\\t(Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:admissible-size}) and $n = 2^m$. Recall from\\\\n    57\\\\tChapter~\\\\\\\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra} the low-degree code\\\\n    58\\\\t(see Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:low-degree-encoding}), which maps each vector\\\\n    59\\\\t$h \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^{n}$, with\\\\n    60\\\\tcoordinates indexed by binary strings of length $m$, to a polynomial\\\\n   255\\\\t\\\\tcircle. Let $A^x = \\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\alpha_a A^x_a$ and\\\\n   256\\\\t\\\\t$B^x = \\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\alpha_a B^x_a$. Then\\\\n   257\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n   258\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tA^x_a \\\\\\\\approx_\\\\\\\\delta B^x_a \\\\\\\\quad\\\\\\\\text{implies}\\\\\\\\quad A^x \\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\delta'} B^x\\\\n   259\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n   260\\\\t\\\\tfor $\\\\\\\\delta' = |\\\\\\\\mathcal{A}| \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\delta$, where the hypothesis is the\\\\n   261\\\\t\\\\tanswer-indexed closeness of Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:povm-distance} and the\\\\n   262\\\\t\\\\tconclusion is the question-indexed closeness of\\\\n   263\\\\t\\\\tDefinition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:approx-question-indexed-operators}.\\\\n   264\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\n   265\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\n   266\\\\t\\\\tExpand\\\\n   267\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n   268\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\Big\\\\\\\\| \\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\alpha_a (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\Big\\\\\\\\|^2\\\\n   269\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\leq\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\Big( \\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\big\\\\\\\\| (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\big\\\\\\\\| \\\\\\\\Big)^2\\\\n   270\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\leq\\\\\\\\, |\\\\\\\\mathcal{A}| \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\E_x \\\\\\\\sum_a \\\\\\\\big\\\\\\\\| (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\psi} \\\\\\\\big\\\\\\\\|^2\\\\n   271\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\leq\\\\\\\\, |\\\\\\\\mathcal{A}| \\\\\\\\cdot \\\\\\\\delta\\\\\\\\,,\\\\n   272\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n   273\\\\t\\\\tusing the triangle inequality followed by the Cauchy--Schwarz\\\\n   274\\\\t\\\\tinequality.\\\\n   275\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n   276\\\\t\\\\n   277\\\\tThe following lemma shows that POVMs that are approximately projective, in\\\\n   750\\\\t\\\\ton average over uniformly random $\\\\\\\\omega$, giving the approximate\\\\n   751\\\\t\\\\tcommutation with $\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Anticom}} = \\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\eps}$.\\\\n   752\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n   753\\\\t\\\\n   754\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{definition}[Expanded line measurements]\\\\n   755\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{def:expanded-line-measurement}\\\\n   756\\\\t\\\\t% In the source this measurement is defined inside the proof of the lemma\\\\n   757\\\\t\\\\t% labelled lem:qld-comm-line-cons (lines 552-556); it is recorded as a\\\\n   758\\\\t\\\\t% standalone definition because Chapter chap:qpbt-combining uses the\\\\n   759\\\\t\\\\t% concrete measurement, not merely its existence.\\\\n   760\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:projective-strategy-setup, def:expanded-state, def:expanded-observables, def:ideg-deg-polynomials, def:low-degree-encoding, lem:pauli-observable-expansion, def:line}\\\\n   761\\\\t\\\\tFor $W \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{X,Z\\\\\\\\}$, a line $\\\\\\\\ell$, and $f \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\deg_{md}(\\\\\\\\ell)$,\\\\n   762\\\\t\\\\tdefine\\\\n   763\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n   764\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_f\\\\n   765\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\,=\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\sum_{\\\\\\\\substack{f',f'' \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\deg_{md}(\\\\\\\\ell) \\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\ f' + f'' = f}}\\\\n   766\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tM^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_{f'} \\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\tau^{W,\\\\\\\\ell}_{f''}\\\\\\\\,,\\\\n   767\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n   768\\\\t\\\\twhere the projector $\\\\\\\\tau^{W,\\\\\\\\ell}_{f''}$, acting on register\\\\n   769\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}'$ (or on $\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}''$, following the register conventions\\\\n   770\\\\t\\\\tof Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:expanded-observables}), is the outcome-$f''$\\\\n   771\\\\t\\\\tprojector of the following projective measurement: measure all $n$\\\\n   772\\\\t\\\\tqudits in the basis $W$, obtaining an outcome $h \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q^{n}$, and\\\\n   773\\\\t\\\\treturn the restriction $f''$ of the low-degree encoding\\\\n   774\\\\t\\\\t$g_h \\\\\\\\colon \\\\\\\\F_q^m \\\\\\\\to \\\\\\\\F_q$\\\\n   775\\\\t\\\\t(see Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:low-degree-encoding}) to the line\\\\n   776\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\ell$. Since $g_h$ has individual degree at most $d$, hence total\\\\n   777\\\\t\\\\tdegree at most $md$, its restriction to $\\\\\\\\ell$ lies in\\\\n   778\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\deg_{md}(\\\\\\\\ell)$, and\\\\n   779\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_f\\\\\\\\}_{f \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\deg_{md}(\\\\\\\\ell)}$ is a\\\\n   780\\\\t\\\\tprojective measurement.\\\\n   795\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\textbf{Self-consistency}) For all $W \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\{X,Z\\\\\\\\}$ and\\\\n   796\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t$r \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q$ (the relation does not depend on $r$), on average over\\\\n   797\\\\t\\\\t\\\\ta uniformly random pair $(\\\\\\\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\\\\n   798\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tdistribution (Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:line-point-dist}),\\\\n   799\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n   800\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_f)_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}'}\\\\n   801\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line}}}\\\\\\\\,\\\\n   802\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_f)_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{B}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}''}\\\\\\\\,,\\\\n   803\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n   804\\\\t\\\\t\\\\twhere the answer summation is over $f \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\deg_{md}(\\\\\\\\ell)$.\\\\n   805\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\item \\\\\\\\label{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons}\\\\n   806\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements I}) On average over\\\\n   807\\\\t\\\\t\\\\ta uniformly random pair $(\\\\\\\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\\\\n   808\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tdistribution,\\\\n   809\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n   810\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_f)_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}'}\\\\n   811\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line}}}\\\\\\\\,\\\\n   812\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_f)_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}'}\\\\n   813\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\ot (\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{f(u)})_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{B}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}''}\\\\\\\\,,\\\\n   814\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n   815\\\\t\\\\t\\\\twhere the answer summation is over $f \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\deg_{md}(\\\\\\\\ell)$.\\\\n   816\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\item \\\\\\\\label{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2}\\\\n   817\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements II}) On average over\\\\n   818\\\\t\\\\t\\\\ta uniformly random pair $(\\\\\\\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\\\\n   819\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tdistribution,\\\\n   820\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n   821\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_{[\\\\\\\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\\\\\\\cdot)=a]})_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}'}\\\\n   822\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\approx_{\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line}}}\\\\\\\\,\\\\n   823\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t(\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a)_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{B}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}''}\\\\\\\\,,\\\\n   824\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\]\\\\n   825\\\\t\\\\t\\\\twhere\\\\n   826\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_{[\\\\\\\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\\\\\\\cdot)=a]} = \\\\\\\\sum_{f \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\deg_{md}(\\\\\\\\ell) \\\\\\\\colon f(u)=a} \\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_f$\\\\n   827\\\\t\\\\t\\\\tand the answer summation is over $a \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q$.\\\\n   828\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{enumerate}\\\\n   829\\\\t\\\\tFurthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also\\\\n   830\\\\t\\\\thold. The proof exhibits the measurements of\\\\n   831\\\\t\\\\tDefinition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:expanded-line-measurement} as witnesses, with\\\\n   832\\\\t\\\\titem~\\\\\\\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons} holding with error $\\\\\\\\eps$ and\\\\n   833\\\\t\\\\titems~\\\\\\\\ref{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons}\\\\n   834\\\\t\\\\tand~\\\\\\\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2} with\\\\n   835\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\delta_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line}}(\\\\\\\\eps) = \\\\\\\\sqrt{\\\\\\\\eps}$.\\\\n   836\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{lemma}\\\\n   837\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{proof}\\\\n   838\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\uses{def:expanded-line-measurement, def:expanded-point-measurement, lem:qld-win-implications, fact:data-processing, fact:add-a-proj2, fact:agreement, def:line-point-dist}\\\\n   839\\\\t\\\\tTake $\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_f\\\\\\\\}$ to be the measurement of\\\\n   840\\\\t\\\\tDefinition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:expanded-line-measurement}.\\\\n   841\\\\t\\\\tItem~\\\\\\\\ref{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons} follows from the fact that the\\\\n   842\\\\t\\\\toriginal line measurements $\\\\\\\\{M^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_f\\\\\\\\}$ are\\\\n   843\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\eps$-self-consistent between registers $\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}$ and $\\\\\\\\mathsf{B}$\\\\n   844\\\\t\\\\tin $\\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\hat{\\\\\\\\psi}}$ (item~\\\\\\\\ref{enu:qld-cons} of\\\\n   845\\\\t\\\\tLemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}), the Pauli projections\\\\n   846\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\{\\\\\\\\tau^{W,\\\\\\\\ell}_{f''}\\\\\\\\}$ are perfectly self-consistent between\\\\n   847\\\\t\\\\tregisters $\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}'$ and $\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}''$, and the data processing\\\\n   848\\\\t\\\\tinequality (Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{fact:data-processing}). For\\\\n   849\\\\t\\\\titem~\\\\\\\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons}: insert the projection\\\\n   850\\\\t\\\\t$M^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_{[\\\\\\\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\\\\\\\cdot)=f'(u)]}$ by\\\\n   851\\\\t\\\\tprojectivity, replace it by\\\\n   852\\\\t\\\\t$(M^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{f'(u)})_{\\\\\\\\mathsf{B}\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}''}$ using the\\\\n   853\\\\t\\\\tline-point consistency (item~\\\\\\\\ref{enu:qld-ld} of\\\\n   854\\\\t\\\\tLemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}) together with\\\\n   855\\\\t\\\\tLemma~\\\\\\\\ref{fact:add-a-proj2}, adjoin the exactly consistent projector\\\\n   856\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\tau^{W,u}_{f''(u)}$ on register $\\\\\\\\mathsf{A}''$, and recombine by the\\\\n   857\\\\t\\\\tconvolution form of Definition~\\\\\\\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement};\\\\n   858\\\\t\\\\tthis gives item~\\\\\\\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons} with error $\\\\\\\\eps$. For\\\\n   859\\\\t\\\\titem~\\\\\\\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2}, the data processing inequality\\\\n   860\\\\t\\\\tdoes not apply directly, since the bound of\\\\n   861\\\\t\\\\titem~\\\\\\\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons} is stated with $\\\\\\\\approx$ rather\\\\n   862\\\\t\\\\tthan $\\\\\\\\simeq$ and its right-hand side is not of the form\\\\n   863\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\Id \\\\\\\\ot B$; instead, a direct computation from\\\\n   864\\\\t\\\\titem~\\\\\\\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons} together with projectivity gives\\\\n   865\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\[\\\\n   866\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\E_{(\\\\\\\\ell,u)} \\\\\\\\sum_{a \\\\\\\\in \\\\\\\\F_q}\\\\n   867\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\bra{\\\\\\\\hat{\\\\\\\\psi}}\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line},W),\\\\\\\\ell}_{[\\\\\\\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\\\\\\\cdot)=a]}\\\\n   868\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\ot \\\\\\\\big( \\\\\\\\Id - \\\\\\\\hat{M}^{(\\\\\\\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\\\\\\\big) \\\\\\\\ket{\\\\\\\\hat{\\\\\\\\psi}}\\\\n   869\\\\t\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\leq\\\\\\\\, \\\\\\\\eps\\\\\\\\,