\\\t2 applied to the conclusion of Lemma~\\\\\\\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines}, whose\\\\n   514\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\simeq_{\\\\\\\\delta_P}$ relation over two independent copies of the\\\\n   515\\\\t\\\\tline-point distribution is precisely the statement that the left-hand\\\\n   516\\\\t\\\\tside of \\\\\\\\eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}, averaged over\\\\n   517\\\\t\\\\t$D_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line}} \\\\\\\\times D_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{Line}}$, is at most $\\\\\\\\delta_P$.\\\\n   518\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n   519\\\\t\\\\n   520\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{lemma}[The sub-line distribution]\\\\n   570\\\\t\\\\t$s_X, s_Z$ chosen accordingly; and again $v'_X, v'_Z$ are the canonical\\\\n   571\\\\t\\\\trepresentatives. In each case a uniformly random point $u$ on $\\\\\\\\ell$\\\\n   572\\\\t\\\\tprojects to a uniformly random point $u_X$ on $\\\\\\\\ell_X$ (resp.\\\\\\\\ $u_Z$ on\\\\n   573\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\ell_Z$), and a case analysis over $j$ shows that the marginal over\\\\n   574\\\\t\\\\t$(\\\\\\\\ell_X, \\\\\\\\ell_Z)$ is a mixture of the required product distributions:\\\\n   575\\\\t\\\\tfor instance, in the axis-parallel case with $1 \\\\\\\\le j \\\\\\\\le m$, the\\\\n   576\\\\t\\\\tmarginal over $(\\\\\\\\ell_X, u_X)$ conditioned on $j$ equals\\\\n   577\\\\t\\\\t$D_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{ALine},j}$, and even conditioned on $(\\\\\\\\ell_X, u_X)$ the line\\\\n   578\\\\t\\\\t$\\\\\\\\ell_Z$ is distributed as $D_{\\\\\\\\mathrm{ALine},i_Z}$ for uniformly random\\\\n   579\\\\t\\\\t$i_Z$; the remaining cases are analogous, differing only in how the\\\\n   580\\\\t\\\\tindices are chosen. This gives Property~2. Property~3 is immediate from\\\\n   581\\\\t\\\\tthe procedure: when $\\\\\\\\ell$ is axis-parallel, both $\\\\\\\\ell_X$ and $\\\\\\\\ell_Z$\\\\n   582\\\\t\\\\tare constructed to be axis-parallel.\\\\n   583\\\\t\\\\\\\\end{proof}\\\\n   584\\\\t\\\\n   585\\\\t\\\\\\\\begin{remark}[Property~3 of the sub-line distribution]\\\\n   586\\\\t\\\\t\\\\\\\\label{rem:qld-subline